            <ArticleTitle>Prognostic significance of being overweight and obese at diagnosis in children with acute lymphoblastic leukemia.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>234-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/MPH.0000000000000056</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>This study tested the hypothesis that being overweight/obese at diagnosis of childhood ALL was related to risk of relapse. In a national cohort of 1033 patients from the UK, there was no evidence that weight status at diagnosis was related significantly to risk of relapse: log-rank test (P=0.90) with overweight and obesity as the exposure (n=917); individual (P=0.42) and stepwise (P=0.96) proportional hazards models, with BMI Z score as the exposure. The study does not support the hypothesis that being overweight/obese at diagnosis impairs prognosis in childhood ALL in the UK.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
